# Patent Claim Draft

**Claim 1:**

A method for selecting reserved tone carrier indices to minimize Peak-to-Average Power Ratio (PAPR) in an Orthogonal Frequency Division Multiplexing (OFDM) system, the method comprising:

(a) receiving, by a processor, an input data sequence to be modulated onto a plurality of data-bearing subcarriers within an OFDM symbol comprising N total subcarriers;

(b) designating a subset of R subcarriers from among the N total subcarriers as candidate reserved tones, wherein the candidate reserved tones are unassigned to the input data sequence;

(c) generating a kernel signal in the time domain by applying an Inverse Fast Fourier Transform (IFFT) to a frequency-domain vector in which the candidate reserved tone positions are populated with unit-magnitude, zero-phase reference values and all remaining data-bearing subcarrier positions are set to zero, wherein the kernel signal characterizes a time-domain impulse response associated with each candidate reserved tone position;

(d) computing, from the kernel signal, a peak-reduction contribution metric associated with each candidate reserved tone index, wherein the peak-reduction contribution metric quantifies an expected reduction in time-domain peak amplitude achievable by assigning a compensating value to said reserved tone index;

(e) selecting an initial subset of K reserved tone indices, where K is less than or equal to R, based on ranking the peak-reduction contribution metrics computed in step (d), and generating a peak-cancelling signal by combining time-domain kernel signals corresponding to the selected initial subset;

(f) combining the peak-cancelling signal with a time-domain OFDM signal generated from the input data sequence to produce a candidate composite signal, and computing a PAPR value of the candidate composite signal;

(g) iteratively updating the selected subset of reserved tone indices by:
   (i) identifying a time-domain sample location exhibiting a peak amplitude in the candidate composite signal;
   (ii) evaluating, for each candidate reserved tone index not currently included in the selected subset, a correlation between the kernel signal associated with said candidate reserved tone index and the identified peak amplitude;
   (iii) exchanging at least one previously selected reserved tone index with a candidate reserved tone index exhibiting a higher correlation value determined in step (ii), thereby forming an updated subset of reserved tone indices;
   (iv) regenerating the peak-cancelling signal using kernel signals corresponding to the updated subset of reserved tone indices;
   (v) recomputing the PAPR value of the composite signal formed from the regenerated peak-cancelling signal and the time-domain OFDM signal; and
   (vi) repeating steps (i) through (v) for a predetermined number of iterations or until the recomputed PAPR value satisfies a predetermined convergence threshold;

(h) selecting, as final reserved tone carrier indices, the subset of reserved tone indices associated with a lowest PAPR value obtained across the iterations performed in step (g); and

(i) transmitting the OFDM symbol using the final reserved tone carrier indices populated with corresponding peak-cancelling values determined from the iterative optimization, together with the input data sequence on the remaining data-bearing subcarriers.

---

**Claim 2 (dependent):**
The method of claim 1, wherein the peak-reduction contribution metric of step (d) comprises a magnitude of the kernel signal evaluated at the sample index corresponding to a peak amplitude location of an initial time-domain OFDM signal generated from the input data sequence.

**Claim 3 (dependent):**
The method of claim 1, wherein step (g)(iii) comprises exchanging the reserved tone index having a lowest peak-reduction contribution metric among the selected subset with the candidate reserved tone index exhibiting the highest correlation value determined in step (g)(ii).

**Claim 4 (dependent):**
The method of claim 1, wherein the predetermined convergence threshold comprises a target PAPR value expressed in decibels, and wherein the iterative updating of step (g) terminates responsive to the recomputed PAPR value being less than or equal to the target PAPR value.

**Claim 5 (dependent):**
The method of claim 1, further comprising encoding the final reserved tone carrier indices and associated peak-cancelling values into side information transmitted to a receiver to facilitate removal of the peak-cancelling signal prior to data demodulation.

**Claim 6 (dependent):**
The method of claim 1, wherein the kernel signal generated in step (c) is precomputed and stored in a memory prior to receiving the input data sequence, and wherein step (c) comprises retrieving the precomputed kernel signal from the memory.

**Claim 7 (dependent):**
The method of claim 1, wherein the number R of candidate reserved tones is selected based on a target data rate reduction constraint, and wherein the number K of selected reserved tone indices is less than R to preserve at least one unused reserved tone for subsequent PAPR optimization iterations.

# METHOD CLAIM

**Claim 1.** A method for reducing peak-to-average power ratio (PAPR) in a wireless communication system, the method comprising:

receiving an input signal comprising a plurality of data-bearing subcarriers within an orthogonal frequency division multiplexing (OFDM) symbol;

selecting, from a set of available subcarriers, a plurality of candidate reserved tone carrier indices designated for peak power reduction, wherein the plurality of candidate reserved tone carrier indices are non-overlapping with the data-bearing subcarriers;

generating a kernel signal corresponding to each candidate reserved tone carrier index of the plurality of candidate reserved tone carrier indices, wherein each kernel signal represents an impulse-like time-domain response associated with a respective candidate reserved tone carrier index;

computing, for each kernel signal, a peak reduction metric based on a correlation between the kernel signal and one or more peak locations identified in the input signal;

comparing the peak reduction metrics computed for the plurality of candidate reserved tone carrier indices to identify a subset of reserved tone carrier indices exhibiting peak reduction metrics satisfying a predetermined optimization criterion;

refining the subset of reserved tone carrier indices by iteratively:
 (i) substituting at least one reserved tone carrier index within the subset with an alternative candidate reserved tone carrier index from the plurality of candidate reserved tone carrier indices;
 (ii) recalculating the peak reduction metric for the substituted reserved tone carrier index based on an updated kernel signal; and
 (iii) retaining the substitution in the subset when the recalculated peak reduction metric indicates an improvement in PAPR reduction relative to a preceding iteration;

determining a set of reserved tone signal values to be applied at the refined subset of reserved tone carrier indices, wherein the reserved tone signal values are determined based on the kernel signals associated with the refined subset and the one or more peak locations in the input signal;

combining the reserved tone signal values with the input signal at the refined subset of reserved tone carrier indices to generate a peak-reduced output signal; and

transmitting the peak-reduced output signal via a wireless transmitter.

---

## Optional Dependent Claims (for completeness)

**Claim 2.** The method of claim 1, wherein computing the peak reduction metric comprises calculating a magnitude of a projection of the kernel signal onto a peak sample of the input signal exceeding a predetermined clipping threshold.

**Claim 3.** The method of claim 1, wherein the predetermined optimization criterion comprises a ranking of the peak reduction metrics, and wherein the subset of reserved tone carrier indices corresponds to a predetermined number of highest-ranked candidate reserved tone carrier indices.

**Claim 4.** The method of claim 1, wherein refining the subset of reserved tone carrier indices further comprises terminating the iterative substitution upon determining that the recalculated peak reduction metric fails to improve beyond a convergence threshold over a predetermined number of successive iterations.

**Claim 5.** The method of claim 1, wherein generating the kernel signal corresponding to each candidate reserved tone carrier index comprises performing an inverse fast Fourier transform (IFFT) operation on a signal having a non-zero value at the respective candidate reserved tone carrier index and zero values at all other subcarrier indices.

**Claim 6.** The method of claim 1, wherein determining the set of reserved tone signal values comprises solving an optimization problem that minimizes a maximum peak magnitude of the peak-reduced output signal subject to a power constraint on the reserved tone signal values.

**Claim 7.** The method of claim 1, further comprising dynamically updating the plurality of candidate reserved tone carrier indices based on channel state information indicating a subcarrier quality metric.
